\par So if the objective is to }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 retain the original calibration to XCAL}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 , then there needs to be a }{\rtlch\fcs1 
\ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 \endash 9.4 dB calibration correction}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  somewhere after incorporating the antenna gain.
\par However, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid466929 NIK LEKIDOT}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  phrase this carefully to the developer:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 If the 2.74 dB median delta was obtained using the JJ model without the RX antenna pattern, and adding the antenna produces a uniform +9.4 dB shift, then the antenna has i
ncreased the XCAL\endash JJ median difference from 2.74 dB to approximately 12.14 dB. Therefore, if the intention is to maintain the original calibration/reference level, a compensating \endash 9.4 dB correction }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\insrsid466929 LEKIDOT}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  indeed be required.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 The important question is why the model needs this compensation.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  The measured XCAL data already represents the si
gnal received through the physical aircraft receiver antenna. If the model now applies the antenna's +9.4 dB gain on top of a model that was previously calibrated to XCAL, it has effectively changed the calibration reference by 9.4 dB.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 But there's an important distinction
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid466929 NIK LEKIDOT}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 not say that the physical antenna gain i
tself should simply be removed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 .
\par Instead, there are two possibilities:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 A. The model is supposed to produce an absolute physical prediction}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
\par Then the +9.4 dB antenna gain should remain, and the fact that the result is now ~12.1 dB away from XCAL means that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 something else in the model/calibration is wrong}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 .
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 B. The model is calibrated against XCAL using the previous 2.74 dB reference}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
\par Then yes, the introduction of the +9.4 dB antenna gain requires a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 \endash 9.4 dB adjustment to preserve that calibration reference}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 .
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 The really interesting finding
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 The fact that the antenna changes the result by }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 exactly +9.4 dB everywhere}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 , rather than producing an azimuth-dependent change, is also important.
\par If the antenna pattern is genuinely being applied along the flight path, you }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid466929 LEKIDOT}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  normally expect:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 RX gain \u8594\'3f changes with aircraft position \u8594\'3f RSRP shape changes.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
\par Instead you're seeing:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 RX gain \u8594\'3f +9.4 dB constant \u8594\'3f same shape.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
\par So }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid466929 NIK LEKIDOT}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  raise }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 two separate questions}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  with the developer:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f31506\fs22\kerning2\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 1.\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls18\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid5129721 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 Why does adding the antenna produce a constant +9.4 dB rather than a positi
on-dependent gain?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507\afs22 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f31506\fs22\kerning2\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 \hich\af31506\dbch\af31506\loch\f31506 2.\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 
Why was the no-antenna JJ result already within 2.74 dB median of XCAL, but the antenna-inclusive result is ~12.14 dB away?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721  
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sa160\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid5129721 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid5129721\charrsid5129721 That is a much stronger and more preci
